## 5. Problems Faced

During development, several challenges may arise when building and evaluating a deep learning model:

- Preparing the dataset and ensuring the input features are suitable for training.
- Applying feature scaling consistently to training and evaluation data.
- Selecting an appropriate neural network architecture.
- Reducing overfitting during training.
- Evaluating the model using a confusion matrix and classification metrics.
- Interpreting false-positive and false-negative predictions.
- Saving and loading the trained model for further use.

These areas require careful implementation, testing, and debugging to ensure that the model performs consistently.

## 6. Conclusion

This project demonstrates the application of deep learning to heart disease prediction using an Artificial Neural Network. The model achieved an accuracy of 98.70%, with high precision, recall, and specificity on the evaluated dataset.

The results suggest that the model can distinguish effectively between cases with and without heart disease in the evaluated data. Further testing on independent datasets and checks for data leakage are necessary to establish its reliability and generalization to unseen cases.

The model is intended for educational and research purposes and should not be used as a substitute for professional medical diagnosis.

